asset-backed securities or abs is a  familiar phrase but what exactly does it  mean imagine a business owner gets a  bank loaned by some real estate if he  can't pay back the loan the bank has a  problem so how does the lender deal with  that risk one option the lender bundles  together these loans into larger  products and sells them off to investors  then it's an asset-backed security or a  BS it sounds like a win-win solution on  the bank side when many smaller loans  are bundled together they're easier to  sell and for buyers asset-backed  securities can be an attractive  investment investors snap them up they  seemed like a safe and lucrative deal  but it turned out that many a BS ha's  were built on a shaky foundation and  were far riskier than they seem  after the global financial crisis the  credit bubble collapsed many of the loan  packages went into default today the ABS  model has fallen into disrepute now the  European Central Bank or ECB wants to  step in the ECB will purchase asset  backed securities that it has deemed  safe from banks the banks will get a  cash infusion in turn they can issue  loans to companies and private  individuals  the ECB hopes that will spur investment  and give the economy a boost but there's  a risk if the debtors default again the  ECB will be liable for the loans and in  the end it's the taxpayers who could be  left holding the bag
 

